@section About VW

@subsection What is VW?

VW is a suite of libraries which provide C++ objects and algorithms
    for building Computer Vision applications and other related
    programs. At its most basic level, it provides numerical
    functionality in the library VNL, simple image and geometric
    objects in the library VW, with the remaining libraries providing
    GUI interfaces using various widget sets and support for a few
    bits of hardware.

@subsection What VW is not

The VW software is not a program - there is no executable, and on its
    own it doesn't do anything particularly interesting. Some examples
(@pxref{(VWExamples)}) are provided which demonstrate how to perform a few simple tasks, but VW is only there to help write applications - the app itself is down to you.



@section Requirements

VW is primarily developed on Redhat Linux (currently version
    9). Despite this we do make some effort to keep as much as
    possible ANSI/Posix compliant so that it should compile and
    install on a variety of Linux distributions without too many
    headaches. Much of it will also work unchanged on Windows (32 bit) using Visual C++ 2003, but full support is a longer term aim. 


The known requirements are:
@itemize
   @item A moderately standards compliant compiler. GCC 2.95 or above
        should be OK, but GCC 3.2 or above is preferable. On windows
        we are looking at Dev Studio 7 (2003). Templates are used extensivley,
so any compiler that has problems with templates (which Microsoft historically seems to think optional) will have trouble.
@end itemize

Each of the VW libraries may have further requirements (eg particular
hardware or GUI widget sets - see below).

@section Obtaining VW

The VW libraries are available from the Active Vision Lab CVS archive using
@code{cvs checkout VW3x} (where x is the current version number). Alternatively source and pre-compiled
packages may be available from the Active Vision Lab software page @uref{http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/ActiveVision/Software}.

@section The libraries

VW comes as a set of libraries, and these are described in the different
chapters of this document. Here is a quick overview of what each does:

@subsection Main libraries

The first two libraries are the heart of the suite, required for most
things:
@itemize
   @item @strong{VNL}: Numerical objects and algorithms - matrix and
                       vector classes, SVD and QR decomposition,
                       non-linear minimisation etc.
   @item @strong{VW}: Image and image feature objects, simple image processing and base
                      classes for displays and image acquisition objects. Most of
                      these have no pre-requisites, but the geometric algorithms
                      make heavy use of the numerics library. Requires @strong{VNL}.
@end itemize

@subsection GUI libraries

For actually displaying the output and building the interfaces for  your
fantastic programs some libraries are provided that make use of various
widget sets:
@itemize
   @item @strong{VWX}: Provides a display class that opens an X window and provides the
                       usual interactive clicking, dragging etc
                       features. Requires @strong{VW,XFree86}.
   @item @strong{VWGL}: Provides a 3D display and some objects to draw
                        on it. You must have a working installation of both OpenGl (plus
                        headers) and the GL Utility Toolkit
                        (GLUT). Requires @strong{VNL,VW,OpenGL,GLUT}.
   @item @strong{VWGTK}: Provides a display class that opens a window and provides the
                         usual interactive features. Also provides a similar display
                         that can be embedded as a widget, and other widgets that
                         interface to various VW objects. The C++ GTK
wrappers use the SigC++ library for their callback handling. Requires
                         @strong{VNL,VW,VWGL,Gtk--,GtkGL--,SigC}.
   @item @strong{VWWx}: Provides a display class that opens a GTK window and provides the
                         usual interactive features. Also provides a similar display
                         that can be embedded as a widget in a WxWidgets interface, and other widgets that
                         interface to various VW objects. Standard WxWidgets events are provided for these widgets. Requires
                         @strong{VNL,VW,VWGL,WxWidgets (with GL canvas)}.
   @item @strong{VWXforms}: Provides a display class that opens an
                            XForms window and provides the usual
                            interactive features using the XForms event
                            queue. This is not used anymore since XForms is pretty much
                            dead. Requires @strong{VW,XForms}.
@end itemize


@subsection Hardware libraries
The hardware libraries provide for image capture from a couple of
devices as well as control of some robotic equipment:
@itemize
   @item @strong{VWMeteor}: Provides an image acquisition system for the Matrox Meteor (version 1)
          frame grabber. This library can be compiled without the
          card being present. Requires @strong{VW}.
   @item @strong{VWFirewire}: Provides an image acquisition system for IEEE1394 (Firewire) digital
          cameras (ie. not DV). This library cannot be compiled
          without both libraw1394 and libdc1394, which provide the
          low-level access to the Firewire device under Linux. Requires @strong{VW,libdc1394}.
   @item @strong{VWYorick}: Support for controlling the Yorick series of
Active Vision Platforms (see http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/ActiveVision/Research/Yorick"). Requires @strong{VW}.
   @item @strong{VWGTI}: Support for controlling the GTI mobile robot (see http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/ActiveVision/Research/GTI"). This cannot be compiled on a machine that doesn't have both the transputers and the relevant driver code installed, since the
compilation process attempts to download the control program onto the transputers. Requires @strong{VW}.
@end itemize




